<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirza Ghalib</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Asadullah Khan Ghalib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirza Ghalib was the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Persian poet of the late Mughal era, best known for his ghazals. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, he lived most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, married into the family of the nawabs of Loharu, and served as poet to the last Mughal emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-ii">Bahadur Shah Zafar</a>. His letters and his account of 1857 record the destruction of Delhi after the Indian Rebellion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirza-ghalib.jpg" alt="Mirza Ghalib" width="290" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirza_Ghalib_photograph_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mirza Ghalib was born in Kala Mahal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, into a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> that had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. His paternal grandfather, Mirza Qoqan Baig, a Seljuq Turk, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from Samarkand, worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and finally settled in Agra. His father, Mirza Abdullah Baig, married Izzat-ut-Nisa Begum, an ethnic Kashmiri, and served the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>; he died in a battle in 1803 at Alwar when Ghalib was a little over five. His uncle Mirza Nasrullah Baig Khan raised him, but died in 1806 after falling from an elephant.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> was his first language, though Persian and Turkish were also spoken at home. He began composing poetry at eleven. At fourteen he met Abdus Samad, a newly converted Muslim from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> who had been a Zoroastrian named Hormuzd, and who stayed with him for two years teaching him Persian, Arabic, philosophy and logic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Until 1827 Ghalib received a monthly share of his uncle's government pension, and he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to petition the Governor-General to keep it. In 1850 the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-ii">Bahadur Shah Zafar</a> gave him the title of Dabir-ul-Mulk, secretary of state, with the addition of Najm-ud-daula, star of the state, and the title Mirza Nosha, which marked his incorporation into the nobility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1854 he became the emperor's poet tutor, and he was also tutor to the emperor's eldest son, Prince Fakhr-ud Din Mirza, and royal historian of the Mughal court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ghalib wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Persian. His Persian Divan is at least five times longer than his Urdu one, but his fame rests on his Urdu poetry. Before him the ghazal was chiefly an expression of anguished love; Ghalib took up philosophy and the travails and mysteries of life, widening its scope. His first pen name was Asad, from his given name Asadullah Khan, and he later took Ghalib, meaning all-conquering.</p><p>He was also a gifted letter writer. Before him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> letters were highly ornamental, and he made his talk like conversation, sometimes writing just the name of the person addressed. His letters gave a foundation to easy and popular Urdu prose, and Ralph Russell translated them in The Oxford Ghalib. He followed Shia theology and wrote verses in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, and he placed more weight on seeking God than on ritual. A large part of his poetry consists of naʽats, poems in praise of Muhammad, among them the Persian Abr-i gauharbar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ghalib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Mirza Ghalib</b> married into the family and was closely tied to its Delhi household.</blockquote><a href="/state/loharu-state">Loharu State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kala Mahal. Died in Ghalib ki Haveli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.65,77.23&amp;year=1843" aria-label="Ghalib ki Haveli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Mirza Ghalib"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4049 5062h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4049 5062h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=28.65,77.23&amp;year=1843">What was here in 1843? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
